Drives runtime/gc/gcworld.bit's stop-the-world World/Mutator registry (#1575)
through its own entry points, not rooting. The registry lives in a raw `[]i64`
`world` slice and every operation on it is `@nosplit` — by definition it never
polls for the collector — so no managed allocation happens and the host (Zig)
collector legitimately never runs. The program is also straight-line (no loop),
so codegen emits `bit_rt_safepoint` nowhere and a stack walk would observe
nothing either way. Its evidence is the linked binary's atomic verdicts —
whether `worldRendezvous` proceeds past a blocked mutator and abandons on a
running one — which is exactly the ABI.md §5 property the host collector running
is beside the point for. The `world` slice is held live in `main` regardless,
which is what keeps the raw pointers into it valid under BIT_GC=stress.
